-- A user-defined infix operator is admitted as an operand of the built-in
-- operator chain (AND / OR / '..' / comparisons / arithmetic), binding
-- tighter than every built-in operator — the same grouping that prefix
-- application has always had. Before this, an infix call was admitted only
-- at the outermost level of an expression or inside parentheses; see
-- SET-OPERATORS-SPEC §17.3 ("Style 3"), whose forms this file pins.

§ `Style 3 — distributed predicates, infix + dittos`

DECLARE Person
    HAS name                 IS A STRING
        `state of residence` IS A STRING

alice MEANS Person WITH name IS "alice", `state of residence` IS "NY"
carol MEANS Person WITH name IS "carol", `state of residence` IS "NJ"
dave  MEANS Person WITH name IS "dave",  `state of residence` IS "CT"
erin  MEANS Person WITH name IS "erin",  `state of residence` IS "DE"

GIVEN p IS A Person
      s IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
p `resides in` s MEANS p's `state of residence` EQUALS s

-- The two-element form: infix operands on both sides of OR, no parens.
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may apply` p MEANS
       p `resides in` "NY"
    OR ^ ^            "NJ"

-- The four-element form: '..' asyndeton rows, dittos carrying the repeated
-- subject and operator; OR uttered once, where grammar compels it.
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may apply broadly` p MEANS
       p `resides in` "NY"
    .. ^ ^            "NJ"
    .. ^ ^            "PA"
    OR ^ ^            "DE"

#EVAL `may apply` alice
#EVAL `may apply` carol
#EVAL `may apply` dave
#EVAL `may apply broadly` erin
#EVAL `may apply broadly` dave

§ `Bare-word operators and mixed built-in forms`

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x SCALEDBY y MEANS x TIMES y

-- Backticks are not load-bearing: a bare-word infix operator is admitted
-- under built-ins the same way, binding tighter than PLUS and EQUALS.
#EVAL 10 PLUS 2 SCALEDBY 3
#EVAL 2 SCALEDBY 3 PLUS 10
#EVAL 2 SCALEDBY 3 EQUALS 6 AND TRUE

-- A built-in comparison as the other operand of OR: the chain binds
-- tightest, then GREATER THAN, then OR.
GIVEN p IS A Person
      n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`qualifies` p n MEANS
       p `resides in` "NY"
    OR n GREATER THAN 3

#EVAL `qualifies` dave 5
#EVAL `qualifies` dave 2

§ `Controls that must stay green`

-- Prefix spelling (was always admitted).
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may apply prefix` p MEANS
       `resides in` p "NY"
    OR `resides in` p "NJ"

-- Parenthesized spelling (was always admitted).
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may apply parens` p MEANS
    (p `resides in` "NY") OR (p `resides in` "NJ")

#EVAL `may apply prefix` carol
#EVAL `may apply parens` carol
#EVAL `may apply prefix` dave

§ `Declared fixity composes with built-in operators`

@infixl 6
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x `plusop` y MEANS x PLUS y

@infixl 7
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x `timesop` y MEANS x TIMES y

-- The declared-fixity chain re-associates (timesop binds tighter) and the
-- whole chain is one operand of the built-in operator on either side.
#EVAL 1 `plusop` 2 `timesop` 3
#EVAL TRUE OR 1 `plusop` 2 `timesop` 3 EQUALS 8
#EVAL 1 `plusop` 2 EQUALS 3 AND 2 `timesop` 3 EQUALS 6

§ `Same-line discipline for operand chains`

-- A chain keyword in operand position must be on the operand's own line;
-- a next-line keyword is NOT absorbed (it would routinely be the next
-- declaration's head). This is why the following top-level definition,
-- whose name is also an operator in scope, parses as a definition:
qq MEANS 1 PLUS
2

GIVEN r IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`plusop-shadow` r MEANS r PLUS 100

#EVAL qq
